rachel: FYI Harbor onboarding — they hit a burst of 429s right after rotating ephemeral keys. Pasting a short trace and their init pattern.
rachel: trace:
```
2026-05-05T10:03:05Z WARN  token exchange status=429 path=/v1/tokens/ephemeral origin=ci-runner-23
2026-05-05T10:03:06Z ERROR request failed status=429 path=/v1/chat/completions client=harbor-onboard
2026-05-05T10:03:06Z WARN  concurrent token requests=72 origin=eu-west-2
```
rachel: They spawn ~72 parallel workers in CI that fetch ephemeral from our endpoint and then immediately call chat. Using js-sdk v1.5.2 with default init.
diego: infra alert from infra-bot: 
infra-bot: [alert] rate_limit_spike account=harbor region=eu-west window=00:00:10 peak_rps=230 baseline_rps=16 id=rl-20260505-08
diego: sounds like a rotate-driven fanout. Immediate recommendations: 1) add a tiny server-side token broker or a shared 2s cache in CI; 2) cap concurrent init to ~8 and add randomized backoff in init wrapper; 3) we can open a short 90s elevated priority window to let inflight requests clear while they patch.
rachel: copying the snippet they gave (CI):
```js
// executed by each worker
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.EPH_KEY });
await client.chat.create({ model: 'redwood-lite', messages: [...] });
```
sam: customer comms I can send: "We observed a short 'stampede' when your keys rotated. Quick wins: deploy a minimal broker that caches identical ephemeral exchanges for ~2s, or add an in-process token cache + gate init concurrency to ~8 with jittered backoff. We'll temporarily raise priority for 90s to drain inflight traffic." OK to proceed?
diego: yes. infra: I'll enable short coalescing for account=harbor when concurrent preflight > 40 in 10s. Rule rl-20260505-08:coalesce, window=120s. Note: coalescing reduces 429s but can add small tail latency.
rachel: sample broker pattern I pasted to customer (Node):
```js
// minimal broker caches identical requests for 2s
const cache = new Map();
app.get('/broker/ephemeral', async (req, res) => {
  const key = `ephemeral-${req.query.user}`;
  const entry = cache.get(key);
  const now = Date.now();
  if (entry && now - entry.ts < 2000) return res.json(entry.token);
  const token = await exchangeLLForEphemeral(process.env.LL_KEY);
  cache.set(key, { token, ts: now });
  setTimeout(() => cache.delete(key), 2100);
  res.json(token);
});
```
sam: also suggested client-side init wrapper (js):
```js
const sem = pLimit(8); // cap concurrency
async function safeInit(fn) {
  return sem(() => retry(fn, { retries: 5, minTimeout: 100, factor: 2, randomize: true }));
}
```
diego: applied infra change rl-20260505-08:coalesce active. I'll also provision a temporary soft quota bump for account=harbor for 90s.
rachel: customer replied: "rolling broker canary now, will report metrics in a couple minutes" :wave:
sam: after broker canary + concurrency cap we should expect >90% drop in 429s within ~90s. I'll draft a short renewal blurb summarizing recommended rotation practices (proxy pattern, concurrency limits, staged rotation) and add it to their account pack.
diego: monitoring: watch token exchange error rates and tail latency for next 30 minutes. If tail latency rises >5% persistently, we can remove coalescing and rely on quota bump + customer patch.
rachel: update: canary shows 89% reduction in 429s after ~75s, logs look stable. Customer asked for a one-paragraph "rotation guidance" for their renewal pack. sam 999please drop that here and we'll close after 24h stable. :thumbsup:"
